course: dictionary
lesson: 13379
title: Hazy: not clear
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some things are hard to see clearly. Hazy means not clear, as if a thin mist, smoke or dust is covering it. «The hills were hazy in the summer heat.» You can picture the hills, but they look soft and blurred, like they are behind a veil.
explain: Hazy can also describe a memory or an idea. «I have a hazy memory of my first day at school.» The memory is there, but the details are blurry and hard to pick out. So the word works for things you see and for things you think about.
explain: Look at the clues around the word. Ask yourself: is something misty, blurry or unclear? If yes, hazy probably fits. Then swap in your meaning, such as «The hills were blurry in the summer heat», and check that the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Hazy means not clear, because of mist, smoke, dust or heat in the air.
fact: «Hazy» can describe a sky, a view or sunshine: «hazy sunshine» is soft, misty light.
fact: «Hazy» can also mean vague in your mind: «a hazy memory» is one you cannot recall clearly.
fact: «Hazy» can mean not sure: «I am a bit hazy about the rules» means you do not fully understand them.
fact: Hot days, bushfire smoke and pollution can all make the air look hazy.
exercise: «The hills were hazy in the summer heat.» What does hazy mean here? | Cold and icy | *Not clear, as if covered by a thin mist | Very loud and noisy | Bright and sparkling like new glass
exercise: «I have a hazy memory of my first day at school.» What does hazy suggest about the memory? | *Vague, not clear in my mind | Written down in a book | Long and boring | Happy and bright
exercise: Which word is the opposite of hazy? | *clear | blurry | dusty | misty
test: «Sam was hazy about the rules of the game.» What does hazy mean here? | *Not sure or confused | Very fast and exciting | Wet from the rain | Loud and cheerful
test: Ella says, «The air was hazy with smoke from the bushfire.» What is the smoke doing? | Making the air cold and fresh | Making the sky very dark and black | *Making the air look blurry and unclear | Making the sun shine brighter
exam: Which sentence uses hazy correctly? | The hazy photo was sharp and clear, so we could see every face | The hazy cup was full of hot tea | Hazy is the name of a big house on the hill | *The hazy photo was blurred, so we could not see the faces
